WEBVTT

00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:30.090
This is a full week, and it runs about forty minutes, well under the usual hour. The research this week was strong on heat, altitude and ageing, and thin on concurrent training, so the hybrid segment is short and says so.

00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:43.040
Here is what is coming. An Ethiopian double in the heat in Hong Kong, eight hundred and fifty three disqualifications at the Behobia, and a Valencia Marathon that has lost its only sub two hour man.

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:56.990
In the news, the United States regulator adds an eye warning to every G L P one drug, a California law that names creatine, and a sports science journal whose editor published papers with references that do not exist.

00:00:56.990 --> 00:01:15.365
In the science, a lactate method that finally lines up with critical speed, reference values for female masters athletes, and a careful estimate of what altitude costs a distance runner. Then a study showing that heat cuts how much of a carbohydrate drink the body burns, and that acclimation does not give it back.

00:01:15.365 --> 00:01:25.740
We finish with RED S on the Fast Talk podcast, three strength papers, and a wearable oximetry study that looked for a skin tone effect and found no significant one.

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:45.945
The Hong Kong Zhuhai Macao Bridge Half Marathon, a World Athletics Platinum Label race, was run on Sunday the fifteenth in hot, humid conditions, with temperatures reaching twenty eight degrees Celsius on an undulating course.

00:01:45.945 --> 00:02:05.870
According to the World Athletics report, Addisu Gobena of Ethiopia won the men's race in one hour, one minute and thirty five seconds, fifty six seconds clear of his compatriot Aliyi Kasim, who ran one hour, two minutes and thirty one seconds on his half marathon debut. Kenya's Alex Lenalepisho, also on debut, was third.

00:02:05.870 --> 00:02:20.595
Gobena is twenty two, and broke the Sydney Marathon course record in August with two hours, four minutes and forty two seconds. Selemon Barega, the twenty twenty one Olympic ten thousand metres champion and one of the favourites, did not finish.

00:02:21.245 --> 00:02:32.870
The women's race was closer. Wede Kefale of Ethiopia won in one hour, nine minutes and four seconds, seven seconds ahead of Glenrose Xaba of South Africa, with Fantu Worku third.

00:02:32.870 --> 00:02:50.095
It was Kefale's first half marathon win in six attempts, and she came in with a best of one hour, five minutes and twenty one seconds, so she won nearly four minutes outside her best; my inference is that most of that gap is the heat. The defending champion, Grace Nawowuna, did not finish either.

00:02:50.745 --> 00:02:59.320
In Spain, the Behobia San Sebastián, a twenty kilometre point to point race run on the eighth of November, has published its numbers.

00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:15.670
The organiser's own report says twenty nine thousand, three hundred and one people entered, twenty seven thousand, one hundred and seventy two started, twenty six thousand, nine hundred and forty seven finished, and of the finishers, eight hundred and fifty three were disqualified.

00:03:15.670 --> 00:03:28.245
Marathon Handbook, citing Spanish reporting, says the main cause is starting before the time printed on the bib, which the rules punish automatically through the chip timing. The race has nineteen start groups.

00:03:28.245 --> 00:03:48.245
Marathon Handbook gives the trend as nine hundred and fifty eight disqualified in twenty twenty four, nine hundred and eighteen in twenty twenty five, and eight hundred and fifty three this year. The organisers published only the total, so nobody outside the timing company knows how many of those were wave jumpers and how many were bib swaps.

00:03:48.245 --> 00:03:58.095
Javi Guerra won the men's race in one hour, one minute and thirteen seconds, and Katherine Tisalema won the women's in one hour, eight minutes and fifty eight seconds.

00:03:58.745 --> 00:04:17.045
The Valencia Marathon on the sixth of December has lost its headline. World Athletics confirmed on the thirteenth that Yomif Kejelcha, who ran one hour, fifty nine minutes and forty one seconds on his London debut in April, has withdrawn through injury. Neither he nor the organisers have said what the injury is.

00:04:17.045 --> 00:04:32.395
Marathon Handbook points out that the fastest official best left in the men's field is John Korir's two hours, two minutes and twenty four seconds from winning Valencia last year. He ran faster in Boston, but Boston's point to point course is not record eligible.

00:04:32.395 --> 00:04:42.395
Valencia attaches a bonus of one million euros to a world record, and on paper no man in the field is near it. The women's race is the more interesting one now.

00:04:42.395 --> 00:05:03.845
Agnes Ngetich, the world ten kilometre record holder and this year's world cross country and half marathon champion, makes her marathon debut against Fotyen Tesfay, who ran two hours, ten minutes and fifty one seconds in Barcelona in March, the fastest women's debut on record. Joyciline Jepkosgei and Korir return as defending champions.

00:05:04.495 --> 00:05:19.345
In triathlon, the Dubai T one hundred was raced on the fourteenth. The World Triathlon report says Britain's Georgia Taylor Brown won the women's race by ten seconds from New Zealand's Nicole Van Der Kaay, with Switzerland's Julie Derron third.

00:05:19.345 --> 00:05:35.495
That moves Taylor Brown to the top of the series on ninety six points, with Derron on ninety and Van Der Kaay on sixty three, before the final in Qatar on the eleventh and twelfth of December. Taylor Brown described cramping on the last bike lap and having to let the group go.

00:05:35.495 --> 00:05:43.295
In the report's words, quoting her, "I just have to try and spin my legs out and try and hopefully it will flush through and it did."

00:05:43.945 --> 00:06:00.370
Two calendar items. World Athletics published the Beijing twenty twenty seven World Championships timetable on the twelfth: ten days from the tenth to the nineteenth of September, with the women's marathon on Sunday the twelfth and the men's on the final morning, Sunday the nineteenth.

00:06:00.370 --> 00:06:10.745
And U T M B announced on the fourth that Wildstrubel in Switzerland and Canyons in California become World Series Majors in twenty twenty seven, making six.

00:06:16.575 --> 00:06:35.550
The biggest regulatory item of the week. On the ninth of November the United States Food and Drug Administration, the F D A, required every maker of a G L P one receptor agonist, the drug class that includes semaglutide, to add a warning about non arteritic anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy.

00:06:35.550 --> 00:06:45.750
That is a rare condition that causes sudden, painless loss of vision in one eye, sometimes permanently. The agency's own summary sets out three lines of evidence.

00:06:45.750 --> 00:07:03.950
A pooled analysis of one hundred and twenty six trials, covering one hundred and thirty four thousand, two hundred and fifty three patients, found ten probable cases in people taking the drugs and five in comparators, an incidence of six point four per hundred thousand person years against three point eight.

00:07:03.950 --> 00:07:25.675
Second, a study in the Sentinel database of adults with type two diabetes found a hazard ratio of one point four three, with a confidence interval of one point zero two to two point zero one, against D P P four inhibitors, but no significant difference against S G L T two inhibitors, at one point one nine, with an interval crossing one.

00:07:25.675 --> 00:07:42.600
Third, the agency found no mechanism in the animal data. Put plainly, the absolute risk is small, the trial analysis rests on fifteen events, and the comparison that matters depends on what you compare against. The F D A still judged the signal consistent enough to label.

00:07:43.250 --> 00:08:02.100
Next, a California law that the strength world is now discussing. Assembly Bill twenty thirty was signed on the twenty eighth of September. I read the chaptered text this week. It bans selling or giving away an over the counter diet pill, or a dietary supplement for weight loss or muscle building, to anyone under eighteen.

00:08:02.100 --> 00:08:20.300
Shops must ask for identification where a buyer's age is not clear, and online sellers must verify age against a database or require an adult signature on delivery. The penalty is a civil fine of up to five hundred dollars per violation. The interesting part is how a court decides whether a product counts.

00:08:20.300 --> 00:08:42.625
The law lists factors a court may consider, and the first is whether the product contains an F D A approved weight loss or muscle building ingredient, a steroid, or, and I quote the list, "Creatine, green tea extract, raspberry ketone, garcinia cambogia, or green coffee bean extract." The other factors are about labelling, marketing, and even where a shop puts the product on the shelf.

00:08:42.625 --> 00:08:56.225
So creatine is named, but as one factor, not as an automatic ban; a tub of creatine sold with no muscle building claims is a harder case than one shelved next to fat burners. The bill text names no start date.

00:08:56.225 --> 00:09:14.900
California statutes normally take effect on the first of January after they are signed, so my inference is the first of January, twenty twenty seven. One caution, in my view: creatine monohydrate is one of the best studied supplements in sport, and this law groups it with products that have nothing like that evidence base.

00:09:14.900 --> 00:09:19.775
That is a policy choice about minors, not a finding about creatine.

00:09:20.425 --> 00:09:27.925
Eric Trexler and his co-host Lauren discussed the law on Front Page Fitness, an episode titled Carded For Creatine.

00:09:27.925 --> 00:09:43.350
On the podcast, one host read the bill's ingredient list and pointed out that the marketing factor, any label implying a product will help maintain or increase muscle or strength, would catch most protein powders as well, which matches my reading of the text.

00:09:43.350 --> 00:10:01.000
Their broader view, put in my words: teenagers do not need many supplements, but legislating against creatine and whey protein while prescription weight loss drugs and testosterone are a few clicks away online is an odd set of priorities, and it risks feeding the idea that creatine is dangerous.

00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:07.050
That is commentary from the podcast, not evidence; the evidence point is the one I made above.

00:10:07.700 --> 00:10:28.100
A follow up to a story from last week's episode. After the Santa Barbara Half Marathon on the eighth, about thirty runners were treated and twenty six went to hospital. Marathon Handbook, citing the hospital operator Cottage Health speaking to the local station K E Y T, reports that all twenty six had been discharged by Tuesday the tenth.

00:10:28.100 --> 00:10:47.150
Police are keeping what they called a suspicious circumstances investigation open, the district attorney says it will act only if a crime is confirmed, and no agency has confirmed a cause. The county health department considers heat the most probable explanation, and its inspectors found the aid station water clean and sealed.

00:10:47.150 --> 00:10:51.800
That is where it stands: unexplained, with heat the leading candidate.

00:10:52.450 --> 00:11:10.075
A research integrity story inside sports science. Retraction Watch reported on the sixteenth that Biology of Sport, the journal of the Institute of Sport in Warsaw, will correct papers by its own editor in chief, Piotr Zmijewski, that contain references to work that does not exist.

00:11:10.075 --> 00:11:29.825
He was an author on nine of the one hundred and twenty papers in the latest edition. One paper on Premier League footballers cites two nonexistent works; another, on the ages of male and female footballers, cites sixteen sources that do not exist and four real papers with invented details. Four more of his papers were flagged on PubPeer.

00:11:29.825 --> 00:11:49.550
Zmijewski told Retraction Watch, "I can confirm that I personally did not use A I in preparing or editing these manuscripts," and blamed an early career researcher who managed a shared reference database. The publisher, Termedia, says it will correct most of the affected articles and has withdrawn one that had not yet appeared in an issue.

00:11:49.550 --> 00:12:03.425
The practical point for anyone reading the sports science literature is that a reference list is no longer evidence that the cited work exists. This digest runs every reference through PubMed and Crossref before air, and this is why.

00:12:09.265 --> 00:12:25.615
Start with a long running argument about thresholds. Maximal lactate steady state, the highest speed at which blood lactate stays stable, and critical speed, the asymptote of the speed duration curve, are both offered as the boundary between the heavy and severe intensity domains.

00:12:25.615 --> 00:12:43.165
In running studies they usually disagree, with the lactate steady state coming out slower. A paper in the International Journal of Sports Physiology and Performance, from Ozkaya and colleagues with Mark Burnley and Andrew Jones, proposes that the disagreement comes from how the steady state is defined.

00:12:43.165 --> 00:12:56.840
The traditional rule is a rise of less than one millimole between ten and thirty minutes. Their individualised version plots the rate of change of lactate against speed across five constant speed runs and finds a breakpoint.

00:12:56.840 --> 00:13:18.840
In eight endurance runners, the traditional method gave thirteen point two kilometres an hour, the individualised method fourteen point zero, and critical speed fourteen point zero. The two agreed almost exactly, with a bias of minus zero point zero two kilometres an hour and limits of agreement from minus zero point one five to plus zero point one.

00:13:19.490 --> 00:13:35.390
Three cautions. Eight runners is a very small sample, and a correlation reported as one point zero in eight people should be read with caution. The method needs five long runs plus four or more runs to exhaustion, which is a lab protocol, not a field test.

00:13:35.390 --> 00:13:53.565
And the authors are, in effect, redefining the steady state until it matches critical speed, so a sceptic would say the match is partly built in. The strongest version of their case, as I read it, is that the one millimole rule is a convention, and that a breakpoint in how fast lactate rises sits closer to the physiology.

00:13:53.565 --> 00:14:10.790
My inference, for coaches who control threshold work by heart rate and perceived effort rather than by lactate: this paper does not change where the ceiling is, but it is a reason to treat a lactate steady state from the traditional rule as a conservative estimate rather than the boundary itself.

00:14:11.440 --> 00:14:28.415
Next, ageing. A systematic review and meta analysis in Sports Medicine, from Shalaya Kipp and colleagues at the Mayo Clinic, pooled sixty nine studies of directly measured V O two max in one thousand, nine hundred and three female masters endurance athletes aged forty and over.

00:14:28.415 --> 00:14:37.715
Pooled values were forty five millilitres per kilogram per minute in the forties, forty in the fifties, thirty five in the sixties and thirty above seventy.

00:14:37.715 --> 00:14:51.040
Each extra year of age was associated with zero point five seven millilitres per kilogram per minute lower V O two max, about eleven point seven percent a decade, and that held across running, cycling and swimming.

00:14:51.040 --> 00:15:03.265
The limitation is in the design: these are cross sectional differences between age groups, not the same women followed over time, and athletes who stay competitive into their seventies are a selected group.

00:15:03.265 --> 00:15:12.540
Still, the authors present these as female specific reference values, filling a gap in a literature where the decline is well documented mainly in men.

00:15:13.190 --> 00:15:28.515
A smaller study in the Journal of Experimental Biology, from Lemineur and colleagues in Nice, asks why older runners also become less economical. Eighteen young and eighteen master athletes ran on a treadmill at two and a half and three metres per second.

00:15:28.515 --> 00:15:46.040
The masters' net cost of running was higher, four point zero two against three point eight one joules per kilogram per metre at the slower speed, and four point two nine against three point eight one at the faster one. They also did less work at the ankle and more at the hip, the familiar shift from distal to proximal.

00:15:46.040 --> 00:16:01.815
But here is the honest part: joint work did not correlate with cost of transport, and the authors say whether these changes cause the higher cost "remains to be established." The tidy story, weaker calves therefore worse economy, was not supported by their own data.

00:16:02.465 --> 00:16:18.415
Now altitude. Alex Hutchinson's Outside column on the twelfth covered a paper in the International Journal of Sports Physiology and Performance by Atle and Simen Guttormsen, of the School of Economics and Business at the Norwegian University of Life Sciences.

00:16:18.415 --> 00:16:35.090
They took six hundred and eighty eight thousand running results from N C A A indoor meets at three hundred and eighty two venues, and used athlete by season fixed effects, so each performance is compared with that athlete's other races that season, with a separate term for banked tracks.

00:16:35.090 --> 00:16:53.890
Sprints were faster at altitude, by zero point three to zero point five percent per thousand metres. Distance events were slower, with the penalty rising from zero point five to zero point nine percent per thousand metres at eight hundred metres to two point seven to three percent at five thousand.

00:16:53.890 --> 00:17:00.890
The crossover between the drag benefit and the oxygen cost fell between four hundred and eight hundred metres.

00:17:00.890 --> 00:17:16.890
Women's eight hundred metre times were penalised nearly twice as much as men's, zero point nine two against zero point five percent, which Hutchinson explains by the women running longer and slower, so they gain less from lower drag and pay more for less oxygen.

00:17:16.890 --> 00:17:28.665
Hutchinson works this out to roughly thirty seconds per thousand metres of altitude over five thousand metres, and reports that the N C A A scrapped its altitude conversions over the summer.

00:17:28.665 --> 00:17:43.065
These are collegiate athletes indoors, with no measure of acclimatisation, so my suggestion is to treat the numbers as a population average, not a personal correction. Hutchinson himself notes that the right correction differs between people.

00:17:43.715 --> 00:17:48.090
A second altitude paper adds sex to the picture, in the lab.

00:17:48.090 --> 00:18:07.240
In the Journal of Physiology, Hansen and colleagues in Odense and Oslo had twenty two trained cyclists and triathletes, ten of them women, ride thirty minute time trials at about four hundred metres and at a simulated two thousand eight hundred metres, with arterial and femoral venous sampling and muscle biopsies.

00:18:07.240 --> 00:18:25.565
Performance dropped in hypoxia by fourteen percent in women and eighteen percent in men. Women rode at a higher fraction of their V O two max at both altitudes and drew relatively more energy from blood glucose. Men showed a large fall in whole body carbohydrate oxidation in hypoxia; women a modest one.

00:18:25.565 --> 00:18:36.165
At the leg, both sexes responded similarly: less glycogen oxidised, no change in fat or blood glucose use. The authors flag their fat estimates as uncertain.

00:18:36.165 --> 00:18:49.890
Put next to the track data, and this is my reading, there are now two independent hints that women and men do not lose the same amount at altitude, though in opposite directions for different reasons, which is a reason not to over read either.

00:18:50.540 --> 00:18:58.240
One more, on prescribing intervals by feel, with Carl Foster as senior author, the researcher behind session R P E.

00:18:58.240 --> 00:19:17.065
In the European Journal of Sport Science, Bok and colleagues had thirteen male physical education students run three three minute intervals either at R P E five on the Borg category ratio scale, or at eighty five percent of maximal aerobic speed. Time spent above ninety percent of V O two max did not differ.

00:19:17.065 --> 00:19:34.590
The R P E session produced lower peak heart rate, one hundred and eighty three point seven against one hundred and eighty seven point nine, lower lactate, nine point six against eleven point seven millimoles, and lower session R P E. The title calls this the same gain for less pain.

00:19:34.590 --> 00:19:48.315
The caveats are real: these are not trained runners, it is one session, and several of the p values sit just under zero point zero five. It is consistent with the case for perceived effort as a prescription tool, not proof of it.

00:19:54.155 --> 00:20:00.405
The fuelling study of the week, and Brady Holmer covered it in his Physiology Friday on the thirteenth.

00:20:00.405 --> 00:20:17.155
In the Journal of Physiology, Tim Podlogar, Gareth Wallis and colleagues at Birmingham and Exeter, with the United States Army Research Institute of Environmental Medicine, asked whether heat acclimation restores the body's ability to burn ingested carbohydrate in the heat.

00:20:17.155 --> 00:20:37.505
Seventeen endurance trained adults, ten men and seven women, cycled for ninety minutes at forty five percent of peak power while drinking glucose at ninety grams an hour, first at twenty degrees, then at forty degrees, both at twenty percent humidity, and then again at forty degrees after nine days of heat acclimation over ten days.

00:20:37.505 --> 00:20:42.980
Dual stable isotope tracers and muscle biopsies tracked where the glucose went.

00:20:43.630 --> 00:21:03.430
Exogenous glucose oxidation, the ingested sugar actually burned, was about twenty seven percent lower in the heat, zero point three seven grams a minute against zero point five one in the cool. After acclimation it was zero point three eight, still suppressed, even though heart rate, core temperature and perceived strain all improved.

00:21:03.430 --> 00:21:16.855
The rate at which the ingested glucose appeared in the blood did not change. What fell was clearance: the tissues took up and burned less of it. The authors conclude that the limit is in peripheral glucose disposal, not in the gut.

00:21:17.505 --> 00:21:36.755
Holmer's reading is careful. He says not to count on acclimation to restore carbohydrate oxidation, but to acclimate anyway for the cardiovascular and thermal benefits. On whether to add thirty percent more carbohydrate in the heat to compensate, his answer is "Probably not," because there is no evidence the extra would be used.

00:21:36.755 --> 00:21:55.505
He also suggests gut training improves carbohydrate oxidation in general, which is a separate claim the study does not test. The limitations: this is cycling at a moderate, steady intensity after an overnight fast, in dry heat, at one dose. Race intensity is higher, and real races are often humid.

00:21:55.505 --> 00:22:04.055
Episode nine covered a related study on cold fluids and glucose oxidation in the heat; this one explains more of the mechanism.

00:22:04.705 --> 00:22:21.780
A review in Sports Medicine from Julien Périard's group in Canberra looked at what elite athletes actually do to prepare for hot competitions. The laboratory guidelines say ten to fourteen consecutive days of heat exposure, sixty to ninety minutes a day, starting at least two weeks out.

00:22:21.780 --> 00:22:35.655
Across twelve observational studies of elite and world class athletes, eleven used sport specific heat exposure, often in conditions as hot as or hotter than competition, and five added passive heating after exercise.

00:22:35.655 --> 00:22:52.955
The authors' main complaint is reporting: protocols and monitoring varied so much, and were described so thinly, that it is hard to judge which ones worked. The usual markers of adaptation, lower exercise heart rate and core temperature and a larger plasma volume, were commonly seen.

00:22:53.605 --> 00:22:57.480
Now a small dose of carbohydrate and durability.

00:22:57.480 --> 00:23:19.005
In the Scandinavian Journal of Medicine and Science in Sports, Iten and colleagues, with Plews and Maunder in Auckland, had thirteen trained cyclists and triathletes ride for two and a half hours at ninety percent of their first ventilatory threshold, with either fifteen grams of carbohydrate an hour or a placebo, and then measured their intensity domain boundaries.

00:23:19.005 --> 00:23:31.980
Fresh, power at the first threshold was one hundred and ninety three watts. After the long ride with placebo it was one hundred and seventy six; with fifteen grams an hour, one hundred and eighty nine, no different from fresh.

00:23:31.980 --> 00:23:49.430
Critical power was three hundred and twelve watts fresh against two hundred and ninety eight with placebo, and with fifteen grams an hour it was no different from placebo. So a very low intake protected the lower boundary, the one most relevant to long steady work, with no evidence it protected the upper one.

00:23:49.430 --> 00:24:05.255
Thirteen riders, and the blood glucose comparisons are labelled exploratory. The practical reading, which is mine: for long sessions done deliberately low in carbohydrate, even a modest intake may preserve the intensity at which easy running stays easy.

00:24:05.905 --> 00:24:18.380
Louise Burke appeared on That Triathlon Show this week answering listener questions on Ironman carbohydrate intake. The public feed carries only the opening few minutes, so I will report only what is in that.

00:24:18.380 --> 00:24:41.580
Asked whether the target should be one hundred and twenty or one hundred and fifty grams an hour, she said, on the podcast, that "most of what we know at the moment is being driven by athlete behavior watching what people are doing in the field rather than having great studies to direct us," and that the answer depends on who is racing: a winner and someone chasing the seventeen hour cut off burn fuel at very different rates.

00:24:41.580 --> 00:24:52.830
That is a useful frame for the Podlogar result. Very high intake targets come largely from field practice, and a lab study now says that in the heat, less of what is drunk gets burned.

00:24:53.480 --> 00:25:13.905
One more fuelling item, a preprint, so not yet peer reviewed. On SportRxiv on the seventeenth, James Steele, Stuart Phillips and colleagues posted an updated systematic review and Bayesian dose response meta analysis of protein intake and changes in lean mass, muscle size, strength and physical function in healthy adults.

00:25:13.905 --> 00:25:32.830
They included ninety five trials with usable data, four thousand, nine hundred and thirty one participants, and rather than assuming a straight line or a fixed breakpoint, they averaged across twenty six possible shapes for the dose response. The associations were modestly positive, especially with resistance training.

00:25:32.830 --> 00:26:03.730
But no supported contrast between a current and a proposed intake produced a conclusively meaningful gain or loss; changes of about zero point two to zero point five grams per kilogram per day from a current intake were generally practically equivalent; and in the authors' words, "We could not identify a clearly optimal intake." Most trials had some concerns or a high risk of bias, adjusting for publication bias pulled the estimates towards zero, and certainty was low or very low throughout.

00:26:03.730 --> 00:26:10.380
That is a sobering read for anyone who treats a precise daily protein target as settled science.

00:26:11.030 --> 00:26:28.580
Last in this segment, bone density and plant based diets, because NutritionFacts dot org published a piece on it this week, and Michael Greger's claim is specific enough to check. He argues that the lower bone density seen in vegetarians and vegans is mostly explained by their smaller body size.

00:26:28.580 --> 00:26:38.105
The key study is Karavasiloglou and colleagues, in the Journal of Nutrition in twenty twenty, using the United States N H A N E S survey.

00:26:38.105 --> 00:26:54.680
After adjusting for body mass index and waist circumference, the difference at the femoral neck and the whole femur was no longer significant, zero point seven seven against zero point seven nine grams per square centimetre, and zero point eight eight against zero point nine.

00:26:54.680 --> 00:27:03.030
But at the lumbar spine a small difference remained and was statistically significant, one point zero one against one point zero four.

00:27:03.030 --> 00:27:21.780
Earlier meta analyses found bone density about four percent lower in vegetarians, and six percent lower at the spine in vegans, in a twenty oh nine Bayesian analysis of nine studies; and a twenty nineteen review of twenty studies and thirty seven thousand participants found higher fracture rates in vegans.

00:27:21.780 --> 00:27:38.680
Greger's case that body size explains the gap is an interpretation of observational data, not a tested result. A fair summary for anyone on a plant based diet: body size explains part of the bone density gap, not all of it, and the spine difference survives adjustment.

00:27:44.860 --> 00:27:55.785
There was no new trial on concurrent training this week that I could find, and I would rather say so than pad the segment. What there was is a case study in hybrid racing from an Olympian.

00:27:55.785 --> 00:28:15.260
Colleen Quigley, eighth in the steeplechase in Rio, entered her first Hyrox in Denver in the open division and won it in one hour, eight minutes and three seconds, more than two minutes clear of a field of over fifteen hundred women, according to Marathon Handbook. A Hyrox is eight one kilometre runs, each followed by a workout station.

00:28:15.260 --> 00:28:30.685
Her running totalled thirty two minutes and eighteen seconds, an average of four minutes and two seconds per kilometre, the fastest of any open woman. On the stations, she ranked sixteenth. She also did six sled pulls instead of four after getting turned around.

00:28:31.335 --> 00:28:47.910
The pro women raced the next day and Rachael Wade won in one hour and fifty seconds. Quigley's time would have placed ninth, but the pro sled is one hundred and fifty two kilograms against one hundred and two, and the wall ball six kilograms against four, so the comparison does not hold.

00:28:47.910 --> 00:29:02.335
What the split shows, and this is my reading, is the shape of the sport: a world class runner can win an open race on the running alone while being ordinary on the stations, which is exactly the gap the strength side of hybrid training is meant to close.

00:29:08.165 --> 00:29:25.440
Fast Talk episode four hundred and forty nine is an hour on relative energy deficiency in sport with Trent Stellingwerff and Kate Ackerman, hosted by Chris Case and Julie Young. Both contributed to the International Olympic Committee's RED S clinical assessment tool, version two.

00:29:25.440 --> 00:29:34.615
I transcribed the episode locally, and this is a machine transcript, so I will paraphrase and attribute it to the podcast rather than to one speaker.

00:29:35.265 --> 00:29:52.265
The core definition, as described on the podcast: RED S is a syndrome of impaired physiological or psychological function in female and male athletes, caused by exposure to problematic low energy availability, meaning prolonged or severe. The word problematic matters.

00:29:52.265 --> 00:30:12.440
The guests distinguish it from adaptable low energy availability, the short term deficit of a hard day or a deliberate weight loss phase, which does not by itself cause the syndrome. They also stress that diagnosis works from the outcomes, the health and performance consequences, rather than from trying to measure energy availability directly.

00:30:13.090 --> 00:30:31.240
Several practical points came through. Bone stress injuries are central: several in the last two or three years is a flag. In men, changes in libido and morning erections can be an early sign, the male counterpart to menstrual disruption. Different symptoms carry different weight and appear on different timelines.

00:30:31.240 --> 00:30:44.465
And early detection changes the outlook: an adolescent corrected at fifteen or sixteen does far better than a woman presenting in her twenties after years of deficiency, who carries a higher risk of future bone injury.

00:30:44.465 --> 00:30:58.015
One guest mentioned working with a young vegetarian man with low bone density, and a past paper on links between specific diets and low energy availability; I have not read that paper, so I will not characterise it.

00:30:58.665 --> 00:31:04.265
This is a clinical consensus view from two of its contributors, not a new trial.

00:31:04.265 --> 00:31:25.140
A scoping review this autumn in Frontiers in Sports and Active Living found thirty two studies using the terms and screening tools inconsistently, with only one using the assessment tool the guests helped write, and awareness of RED S ranging from ten to ninety two percent across samples, which fits the guests' point that practice has not caught up with the definition.

00:31:31.330 --> 00:31:49.380
Three papers. First, how well people judge proximity to failure. In the Journal of Sports Sciences, Holmes and colleagues, with Eric Helms, had thirty two people, nineteen trained and thirteen untrained, do machine chest press and leg extension at seventy five percent of one repetition maximum.

00:31:49.380 --> 00:31:58.455
In one session they called out when they reached two reps in reserve, in the other when they reached an eight on the Borg R P E scale, then kept going to failure.

00:31:58.455 --> 00:32:15.230
Accuracy was the same either way: the mean difference was minus zero point zero three reps for chest press, with a confidence interval from minus zero point five to plus zero point four four, and zero point one six for leg extension. Training status made no difference.

00:32:15.230 --> 00:32:28.430
This is machines and one load, and the paper only says the two scales are equally accurate, not that either is accurate in absolute terms. But it undercuts the idea that one scale is clearly better for prescribing effort.

00:32:29.080 --> 00:32:46.930
Second, how fast to add volume. In the Journal of Strength and Conditioning Research, Enes and colleagues in Brazil used a within subject design in twenty one recreationally trained women: one leg added two weekly sets for the quadriceps every two weeks, the other every four weeks, for twelve weeks.

00:32:46.930 --> 00:33:05.180
Both legs got stronger and bigger, with no detectable difference between them in leg press strength or muscle thickness. Twenty one people, and the authors limit their conclusion to the relatively high volumes they tested. It is a null result about the rate of progression, not evidence that progression does not matter.

00:33:05.830 --> 00:33:17.955
Third, a narrative review in Sports Medicine from Graz on supramaximal accentuated eccentric loading, where the lowering phase uses more than the concentric one rep max and the lifting phase less.

00:33:17.955 --> 00:33:35.555
The authors report meaningful strength gains with as little as two to four sets and three to eight eccentric heavy reps a week, though they suggest trained lifters may need five or more sets. They are frank that direct comparisons with ordinary training are sparse, interventions short and methods varied.

00:33:35.555 --> 00:33:40.330
It is a hypothesis about time efficiency, not a recommendation.

00:33:40.980 --> 00:33:57.680
One supplement paper sits here. In Biological Research for Nursing, Oliveira and colleagues, including Candow and Forbes, randomised forty eight women, twenty six young and twenty two older, to three grams of creatine monohydrate a day or placebo for twenty eight days.

00:33:57.680 --> 00:34:17.180
The creatine groups improved on several cognitive measures, most consistently reaction time, inhibitory control and working memory, with the strongest Bayesian evidence in the older women. The authors call their test battery exploratory and multi domain and say the result needs confirming in hypothesis driven trials.

00:34:17.180 --> 00:34:24.130
Many outcomes, small groups, and baseline creatine intake differed between groups. Interesting, not settled.

00:34:30.320 --> 00:34:49.870
Start with a study this digest watches for: whether wearable sensors work equally across skin tones. In Frontiers in Digital Health, Miller and colleagues at Lehigh University compared four consumer wearable pulse oximeters with two clinical monitors in forty nine adults, at rest, walking on a treadmill and in recovery.

00:34:49.870 --> 00:35:07.620
Skin tone was measured objectively with the individual typology angle as well as the Fitzpatrick scale. Agreement varied by device and activity: the Garmin read about zero point seven two percentage points low during recovery, and the Wellue about one point zero three low on the treadmill.

00:35:07.620 --> 00:35:25.195
After correcting for multiple comparisons, no device showed a significant association between skin pigmentation and error. Just as notable, the Garmin produced readings only eighty two point four percent of the time on the treadmill, and the Apple Watch zero point four percent. Two cautions.

00:35:25.195 --> 00:35:44.695
Forty nine people is a small sample for detecting a skin tone effect, so a null result here is not proof of no bias. And this is walking, not running. In my view the method, an objective colour measure rather than a visual category, is the right one, and in this sample activity and data dropout mattered more than pigmentation.

00:35:45.345 --> 00:36:04.420
From D C Rainmaker, two Suunto items. Citing Helsingin Sanomat, he reports that Suunto, owned since twenty twenty two by Liesheng, which is consolidating its functions in China, notified one hundred and eighteen of its roughly one hundred and sixty remaining staff in Finland that their jobs may go.

00:36:04.420 --> 00:36:23.120
He also notes the price shifts: the Suunto Run two at three hundred and forty nine dollars against two hundred and forty nine for the Run one, and the Race three S at five hundred and ninety nine, which he calls a seventy percent increase over the Race S. His own conclusion is that it will soon be hard to call Suunto a Finnish brand.

00:36:23.770 --> 00:36:40.845
Nike unveiled two shoes on the sixteenth. The Vomero nineteen daily trainer goes on sale in March twenty twenty seven, lighter than the Vomero eighteen with the same stack height. The Vaporfly five is, in Nike's words, "currently a prototype," due in autumn twenty twenty seven.

00:36:40.845 --> 00:36:54.620
Nike claims its new foam is seventeen percent lighter than Zoom X and returns eight percent more energy; those are the manufacturer's numbers, with no independent testing yet. Nike's announcement says nothing about widths.

00:36:55.270 --> 00:37:14.220
On this digest's standing focus on wide fittings, a quick pass through the week's other reviews. Doctors of Running reviewed the ASICS Gel Nimbus twenty nine, the Diadora Nucleo three and the rabbit E Z Chaser, and Northern Runner reviewed the Brooks Ghost Trail two and the Altra Experience Wild three plus Gore Tex.

00:37:14.220 --> 00:37:34.145
I could not confirm a wide fitting sold in the U K for any of them this week. Brooks's U K wide fit page lists several road models in wide and extra wide, but no Ghost Trail, and the ASICS U K site blocked automated access, so the Nimbus twenty nine is unconfirmed rather than ruled out. None gets more than this sentence.

00:37:40.325 --> 00:37:57.700
Gluck's Gym published two review videos since last week: a ranking of adjustable dumbbells on the sixteenth, and a review of the Speediance Gym Nano on the twelfth. Neither could be read: the caption download was refused with a rate limit error, and this digest does not summarise a review from its title.

00:37:57.700 --> 00:38:01.825
Both will be eligible next week if the captions can be fetched.

00:38:08.005 --> 00:38:23.130
A selective round up of what else the sources published. Some Work, All Play episode three hundred and thirty seven discussed the recent re analysis of a nineteen seventy seven V O two max training study, which this digest covered in episode six.

00:38:23.130 --> 00:38:42.230
In the Journal of Strength and Conditioning Research, Michael Rosenblat, Gunnar Treff and Stephen Seiler published a manuscript clarification about a network meta analysis of training intensity distribution by Li and colleagues; it is behind a paywall and I could not read what they clarify, so I note only that it exists.

00:38:42.230 --> 00:39:02.405
A Heart Rhythm paper set Q R S duration norms from nine thousand and eighty four young athletes; the abstract gives no breakdown by ethnicity, so it does not speak to E C G interpretation in Black athletes. Brady Holmer wrote for Marathon Handbook on lactate supplements, mostly revisiting studies this digest covered in episode nine.

00:39:02.405 --> 00:39:23.255
Marco Altini launched a rebuilt H R V four Training Pro web platform. Sigma Nutrition episode six hundred and twenty seven asked whether nutritional biomarkers can settle diet and disease questions. The Running Channel began a doping mini series. John Davis appeared on the TriDoc podcast talking about biomechanical training load.

00:39:23.255 --> 00:39:29.930
And the Sika Strength podcast is publishing again, with a mid October episode after a gap since July.

00:39:30.580 --> 00:39:35.380
That is the week. The archive and the references are in the companion notes.
